  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the electricity meter do?",
      "a": "It measures how much energy your home uses, in kilowatt-hours, so the supplier can bill you. One kilowatt-hour is a 1000 watt heater running for an hour.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a kilowatt-hour?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a consumer unit?",
      "a": "It is the fuse box: the panel where the supply splits into separate circuits, each protected by its own circuit breaker, usually with RCDs to protect people from shocks.",
      "asks": [
        "What is in a fuse box?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are lights on a separate circuit from sockets?",
      "a": "Lights use little current, so they have thinner cables and a smaller breaker, often 6 amps. Keeping them separate also means a faulty kettle tripping the sockets will not leave you in the dark.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do the lights stay on when the sockets trip?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a ring main?",
      "a": "In many UK homes, sockets are wired in a loop called a ring circuit, which starts and ends at the consumer unit. Current can reach each socket by two routes, so the cable can be thinner.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are UK sockets wired in a ring?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is the house wiring connected to earth?",
      "a": "The earth connection gives fault currents a safe path to the ground, so breakers trip quickly and metal parts never stay live.",
      "asks": [
        "What does earthing a house do?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "One kilowatt-hour is the energy used by a 1000 watt appliance running for one hour.",
    "UK ring circuits for sockets are typically protected by a 32 amp breaker."
  ],
